Subject: [PATCH 0001] branding: icons, installer resources, and shell integration
Refs: ADR-0010
Status: UNWRITTEN — header and intent only, no diff hunks. See below.

STATUS
======

This file contains no diff. It is disabled by its `.patch.no` suffix and will not
be applied. Writing hunks against `resources/` and `build/win32/` without having
read the upstream tree at 1.135.0 would produce a patch that fails to apply, and
a plausible-looking patch that fails is worse than a placeholder that says so.

INTENT
======

Replace upstream's visual identity with Adze's, everywhere it is a file on disk
rather than a string in product.json.

`product.json` is generated from apps/ide/branding/product.delta.jq and carries
every *name*, identifier, and URL. This patch carries everything that is a binary
asset or an installer script, which a jq program cannot express.

FILES
=====

  resources/win32/code.ico              application icon
  resources/win32/code_70x70.png        Windows tile assets
  resources/win32/code_150x150.png
  resources/win32/*.ico                 per-file-type document icons
  resources/darwin/code.icns            macOS bundle icon
  resources/darwin/*.icns               per-file-type document icons
  resources/linux/code.png              Linux icon, name from linuxIconName
  resources/linux/rpm/code.spec.template
  resources/linux/debian/control.template
  build/win32/code.iss                  Inno Setup script
  build/win32/*.bmp                     installer wizard imagery

The Inno Setup script is the one interesting file. It reads AppId, mutex names,
and the registry value name from product.json, so this patch should not hardcode
any of them — if it does, the four fresh GUIDs in the delta stop being the single
source of truth and a future GUID change silently fails to take effect.

WHY THIS IS LOW RISK
====================

Upstream edits these files when it rebrands or adds a file-type association,
which is rare. The churn table in ADR-0010 measured the code we build on; these
resource paths are quieter still. Expect this patch to survive most upstream
releases untouched.

The risk that does exist is not merge risk. It is that an icon is missing for one
target and the build fails late, in a packaging task, having already spent forty
minutes compiling.

HOW TO WRITE IT
===============

Assets have to be authored before the patch can exist, and none have been. The
repository has one SVG (apps/vscode/media/adze.svg) intended for the extension's
activity bar, which is the wrong size and the wrong format for every entry above.

  1. Author the icon set from that SVG: .ico with 16/32/48/256 px frames, .icns
     with the Retina variants, and the PNG tile sizes.
  2. Copy them over the upstream paths in a fetched checkout.
  3. git -C apps/ide/vscode diff adze/pristine -- resources build/win32 \
       > apps/ide/patches/0001-branding-icons-and-installer-resources.patch
  4. Rename this file away, or delete it once the real patch exists.

Binary files inside a patch need `git diff --binary`. Without that flag git emits
a "Binary files differ" marker that carries no content and applies as a no-op —
producing a build that silently keeps upstream's icons.
